<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>eu.wohlben.qits</groupId>
        <artifactId>qits-containers</artifactId>
        <version>2026.904.210416</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <!-- What a consumer depends on to CALL this service: the wire records, the four-outcome answer
         and the HttpClient behind them.

         IT DOES NOT DEPEND ON `core`, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE MODULE. A caller wants a few
         records and a client; `core` carries entities, a Flyway lineage and datasource defaults at
         ordinal 100, and every one of those would arrive in qits-ci, qits-workspaces and
         qits-projects the day they depend on this. The wire types are restated here rather than
         shared, which is the same trade the poms make with their duplicated versions.

         TWO DEPENDENCIES AND NO FRAMEWORK. There is no quarkus-arc here and no OIDC extension:
         ContainersClient is a plain class a consumer's own producer turns into a bean, which is
         what lets a daemon with no CDI construct one and what keeps this jar out of the way of a
         consumer's own container. -->
    <artifactId>qits-containers-client</artifactId>
    <name>qits-containers :: client</name>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <!-- Jackson itself, NOT a Quarkus extension and NOT the CDI ObjectMapper. ContainersJson
                 builds its own mapper for the reason CanonicalJson does: a consuming application's
                 ObjectMapperCustomizers must not be able to change what this client puts on a wire
                 another service parses. quarkus-jackson would bring the same jar plus an extension,
                 and an extension in a client jar is arc in every consumer.

                 Version from the Quarkus BOM, so this agrees with whatever the consuming service
                 already has on its classpath rather than pulling a second copy in beside it. -->
            <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- Causation, for two classes: CausationHeader.NAME and CausationScope.current(). The
                 client stamps X-Qits-Causation-Id on every request by hand, exactly as that header's
                 own javadoc prescribes for a caller that speaks java.net.http.HttpClient rather than
                 the REST client — CausationClientFilter is a JAX-RS provider and there is no JAX-RS
                 here to discover it.

                 COMPILE SCOPE, NOT PROVIDED OR OPTIONAL. The eventstream jar is the platform's
                 causation home and it is already a mandatory dependency of every service that would
                 call this one, so nothing new arrives in practice — and the alternative is worse in
                 both directions. Copying the header name into this jar would be a second answer to
                 a question the platform has settled once, and it would silently stop matching the
                 day the name moved. Declaring it `provided` would make it a line every consumer has
                 to restate, and a consumer that forgot would fail with NoClassDefFoundError on its
                 first call rather than at resolution. Same reasoning qits-eventstream's own pom
                 gives for taking qits-db-core at runtime scope instead of leaving it optional.

                 It brings arc and the outbox's extensions along transitively. That is honest rather
                 than free: it is the price of the platform having ONE causation implementation, and
                 a consumer that declares qits-eventstream itself — every one of them does — resolves
                 its own version rather than this jar's. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-eventstream</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <!-- Plain JUnit, no @QuarkusTest. THE SUITE IN THIS MODULE IS PURE UNIT TESTS: outcome
                 mapping against a stub, URL building, header stamping, and the HTTP/1.1 pin. It
                 starts no application, needs no database and reaches no docker, which is what keeps
                 the module as light to build as it is to consume. What the client does against the
                 REAL REST surface is proved in `service`, where that surface is — see
                 ContainersClientWireTest. -->
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
